{"id": "10755", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10755", "title": "El Colorado (Chile)", "text": "El Colorado is a ski resort about 40 km outside of Santiago, Chile.\nUnderstand.\nEl Colorado has restaurants, equipment rental, and ski and snowboard schools.\nGet in.\nEl Colorado is easily accessible from Santiago by road.\nBy bus.\nNumerous companies offer service between different points in Santiago and El Colorado, and a round trip ticket usually costs around 12,000 pesos.\nBy road.\nEl Colorado is only accessible by a narrow mountain route with over 20 switchbacks. Motorists should also be sure to check whether the road is operating in both directions; on weekends during the winter season, the road often operates in one direction (towards El Colorado in the morning, and back to Santiago in the afternoon).\nFees.\nSki lift ticket prices (as of 2021):\nGet around.\nAll parts of the hamlet of El Colorado are accessible by foot. During the season, the ski resort operates a shuttle connecting the hamlet with the mountain base.\nDo.\nSkiing, snowboarding, sun bathing, etc.\nEat.\nEl Colorado ski resort has several restaurants at its base and on the mountain."}
{"id": "10757", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10757", "title": "El Cuco", "text": "El Cuco, also known as \"Oriente Salvaje\" among surfers, is a quintessential beach with dark sands, lined with vendor huts, seafood restaurants and shaggy palms. It is a world-famous surfing destination that is regarded as one of the best in the world, although it is not well known outside the surfing community. Non-surfers will find long stretches of sandy beaches that are lightly visited but with a local beach vibe unspoiled by crowds and development.\nUnderstand.\nThe beaches of El Cuco are known for their black sand. The sandy beaches are formed by ground volcanic rock, which gives it the dark color. The beaches aren't actually black, though they can appear that way when wet. The colors actually range from light gray to dark black and are often mixed with light flecks made up of ground seashells. The sand is fairly soft to walk on but can be quite hot due to the absorption of energy from the bright afternoon sun. \nGet in.\nThe Pan-American highway crosses through El Salvador and is a safe route for entering the country and traveling between San Miguel in the East and San Salvador in the West.\nFrom the San Miguel bus terminal on east 6th Street (6a Called Ote.) there are regular 90-minute bus rides to El Cuco. The buses leave every 30 minutes (or when the bus is full) and cost US$2. You can also get a $30 taxi or some hotels offer a shuttle service from San Miguel to Playa El Esteron via El Cuco.\nGet around.\nIn Playa El Esteron you can find hotels that offer a shuttle service between Intipuca Beach, Playa Las Flores and El Cuco or alternatively you can take a cheap taxi between the neighboring beaches.\nSee.\nConchagua Volcano.\nIf you want to do some hiking and nature observing, the nearby Conchagua Volcano is a good choice. The 1,225 meter high stratovolcano is considered active, with smoking fumaroles, but it hasn't had a real eruption in recorded history. On your visit, you'll likely spot lots of wildlife as you hike up the well-marked trail or ride up in a park vehicle. The lush foliage is home to deer, several types of monkey (including the \"spider monkey\") and a type of agouti that locals call a \"cotuza\"). At the summit there is a platform with outstanding views of the ocean, bays, and nearby hills, not to mention outrageous sunset views. You can set up tents on the summit platform to spend the night.\nEat.\nYou can find the best sea food in Central America fresh from the coast of El Salvador at Playa El Cuco. Local palapa bars and restaurants can be found on beaches in both directions. At least half are usually closed since local owners agree among themselves which days they will open or close. No extra charge for the spectacular sunsets over the Pacific Ocean.\nDrink.\nIntipuca Beach is home to the first microbrewery in El Salvador and offers an array of hand-craft beers by the beach. You can also find several bars and liquor stores in El Cuco or for party-nightlife you can head to Ave. Roosevelt in San Miguel which has numerous bars and clubs open until the early hours of the morning and is also hosts to the biggest carnival in Central America in November."}
{"id": "10759", "revid": "76122", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10759", "title": "El Dorado", "text": "There is more than one place called El Dorado or Eldorado:"}
{"id": "10760", "revid": "2236161", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10760", "title": "El Dorado (Arkansas)", "text": "El Dorado is a city in the Timberlands region of Arkansas.\nGet in.\nGreyhound bus lines offers a daily bus from Little Rock to El Dorado. \nThe South Arkansas Regional Airport at Goodwin Field () has flights are operated by Southern Airways Express from Dallas-Fort Worth International Airport and Memphis.\nGet around.\nThere is no public transportation in El Dorado, but there are some car rental agencies in town. Traffic runs fairly smoothly, so anyone used to driving in small- to medium-sized cities will have no problems getting around.\nDo.\nSouth Arkansas Arboretum, 1506 Mt Holly Rd. Plant specimens representative of the Arkansas West Coastal Plain shown. M-Sa 8AM-5PM, Su 9AM-5PM. Tel: +1 888-287-2757\nBuy.\nDowntown El Dorado has plenty of small boutiques, primarily aimed towards women."}
