{"id": "10900", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10900", "title": "Ely (England)", "text": "Ely is a small, historical cathedral city in Cambridgeshire, England. Ely combines two sides: it is a pocket-sized medieval city that retains much of its medieval character, and at the same time a relaxed and tolerant market town, with increasing links to Cambridge. The \"Ship of the Fens\" dominates the surrounding landscape in much the same way it has done for the past millennium.\nUnderstand.\nBuilt on a chalk hill, the city is thought to be named after the eels in the nearby River Ouse. The hill was once an inaccessible island in the middle of the Fens. It was also the last stronghold of Anglo-Saxon resistance, under Hereward the Wake who hid in the original cathedral until the Normans crossed the Fens in 1071.\nThe surrounding landscape has been transformed from watery marshland to fertile farmland, and nearby Cambridge is continuing to grow, but Ely remains apart. \nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major international airport is London Stansted (), with flights from destinations throughout the UK, Europe, the Middle East and North Africa. Trains by CrossCountry and Greater Anglia link Stansted Airport with Ely. The airport is less than 50 mi from Ely by road.\nAlternatively, Norwich Airport () is around 60 mi away from Ely by road, served by a small number of UK and European flights.\nThe UK's largest international airport at London Heathrow () is around 90 mi away by road, served by a large number of flights from destinations throughout the UK, Europe, Africa, Asia, and North and South America. From the airport, use London Underground Piccadilly line \u00a0PIC\u00a0 to King's Cross St Pancras and change for National Rail Great Northern services direct to Ely, or use Elizabeth line \u00a0ELI\u00a0 to Liverpool Street and change for National Rail Greater Anglia services direct to Ely.\nBy train.\n is the only station in the city. It is on the National Rail network, served by:\nYou must buy a ticket before you travel. You can do this at most stations from a ticket office or machine, or online using the National Rail website or another retailer, like The Trainline.\nBy car.\nEly lies at the junction of two major routes:\nEly is signposted from most major routes nearby, including from London and London Stansted Airport, from Northampton and Ipswich, from Wisbech and Mildenhall and from Huntingdon and Soham.\nMany routes into Ely become congested during the morning and evening \"rush hour\" (peak) periods, particularly near Cambridge, Newmarket and King's Lynn. Some routes flood in the winter, particularly the at Welney.\nMany smaller roads and local routes are poorly surfaced, with unguarded ditches and dykes.\nBy bicycle.\nYou can cycle to Ely from Cambridge. Follow National Cycle Network Route 11 from Cambridge city centre. The route follows the River Cam towpath to Waterbeach, then local roads and bridleways into Ely city centre. It is signposted throughout. Experienced cyclists who are happy to mix with fast-moving motor traffic can alternatively follow then from Cambridge to Ely. Routes are between 16 mi and 25 mi.\nYou can cycle from most surrounding towns and villages using local routes.\nGet around.\nEly's small city centre can easily be traversed on foot. Local villages are served by buses running from Market Street, check the boards or tourist information centre for departures.\nDo.\nTours.\nEly is a compact city and so well-suited to walking tours. Tour providers include the established Visit Ely tours and newer option Elver tours. Travellers who prefer to show themselves around may enjoy Visit Ely's self-guided Eel Trail.\nSleep.\nVarious Bed &amp; Breakfast establishments can be found in the city. A web search should turn up a vacancy. Most are within walking distance from the train station."}
{"id": "10901", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10901", "title": "Ely (Minnesota)", "text": "Ely is a town of 3,400 people (2019) in Northeastern Minnesota. Ely is best known as a popular entry point for the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness, the International Wolf Center, and the North American Bear Center.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe first Europeans to explore the area were fur traders who made their way into the wilderness in search of furs. But it was the Lake Vermillion gold rush that brought the first groups of pioneers to the area in 1865. Although hardly any gold was ever found, it was discovered that the area did contain large deposits of iron ore. Many immigrants arriving in America came to the area later to be known as the Minnesota Iron Range, looking for work.\nWhen the Duluth, Missabe and Iron Range Railway was extended to Ely in 1888, mining operations begsn with the opening of the Chandler Mine. Ore was shipped by rail to docks on Lake Superior in Two Harbors and Duluth. From there it was shipped by lake freighter for processing in Ashtabula and other points in Ohio.\nThe name \"Ely\u201d was chosen in honor of mining executive Samuel B. Ely, a big promoter of Vermilion Range ore who lived in Michigan. (He was not known to have visited the namesake town.)\nEventually eleven mines opened near Ely. In 1967, the Pioneer mine closed. It is on the National Register of Historic Places and its offices now house the Ely Arts &amp; Heritage Center.\nThe first mines were open pit operations, but when the abundant ore was mined out, deep shafts were made to start mining underground. With the need for wooden support beams to keep the tunnels from collapsing, along with the need for lumber to meet the needs of the ever-expanding growth in the area, the logging and milling industry grew. Logging continues in the region, though on a limited scale and only for paper pulp\u2014the major operations virtually disappeared by 1920 when the area's tree reserves were depleted.\nGet in.\nPlan to spend some time getting to Ely. They don't call it the \"End of the Road\" for nothing! If you take a commercial flight to Minnesota, plan to rent a car to get to Ely. It's about four hours from the Twin Cities, or two hours from Duluth. Ely is 100 miles (160 km) north of Duluth, 117 miles southeast of International Falls, and 244 miles (393 km) north of Minneapolis \u2013 Saint Paul.\nState Highway 1 (MN 1), State Highway 169 (MN 169) and County Road 21 (Central Avenue) are the main routes in Ely.\nGet around.\nPrimary highways are wide and well-maintained summer and winter, but secondary county roads in the vicinity of Ely are often narrow, hilly, and have a gravel or dirt surface. The scenic Echo Trail (County Road 116) heading north of town is beautiful, but especially rugged.\nDo.\nIn the winter: Dog sledding, cross country skiing, snowshoeing, and a variety of other winter activities, since the lakes have frozen over and are covered with snow. One place to book winter trips is through Wintergreen Dogsled Lodge\nBuy.\nThe main street of Ely is lined with outfitters, outdoor clothing stores, and restaurants.\nFor all of your outdoor needs, there is Piragis. To save some money, check out their outlet shop, a block away on Sheridan Street. There also is Wintergreen, which also offers outdoor supplies. Steger Mukluks will keep your toes warm. And there are probably two dozen other outfitters and equipment suppliers where you can get good quality and a good selection of most any outdoor stuff you want.\nThere are many fun gift shops as well. Some feature woodsy and down-home items, some have lovely artistic decor items, or furniture, or quirky widgets. There's plenty of fun shopping.\nAlong the main street you also find two dry cleaners.\nThere is a hemp store and an army surplus store. Good food at a variety of restaurants and eateries.\nEly is a small town, it's fun just to walk around the town and explore these places for yourself."}
{"id": "10902", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10902", "title": "Ely (Nevada)", "text": "Ely is in eastern Nevada near the Utah border at the intersection of US Highways 50, 93, and 6.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nU.S. Routes 6, 50 and 93 converge on Ely. To the north is Highway 93, which connects to Interstate 80 at Wells, I-84 at Twin Falls and I-90 at Missoula. Highway 50 heads west from Ely, eventually making its way to Fallon and Carson City on the other end of Nevada. This highway has an alternate route between Fallon and Fernley for easy access from I-80. Highway 6 travels southwest of Ely and connects to Tonopah and the Sierra Nevada region of California. Southeast of Ely there is a concurrency of all three highways; Route 93 quickly branches off south toward Las Vegas, while Highways 6 and 50 share the road all the way to Delta in Central Utah, not far from Interstate 15.\nThe leg from the west of Route 50 is called the \"Loneliest Road in America\" because of the remote areas it goes through.\nBy train.\nThere is no local train service.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest large commercial airports are in Salt Lake City, Utah and Las Vegas, Nevada.\nDo.\nEly has several small casinos that offer slot machines if you want to try your luck, and the Hotel Nevada has live gaming with blackjack and poker tables.\nAt the east end of the city there are a few legalized brothels that will give visitors free tours.\nPhotographers will want to take a stroll downtown for shots of a pretty little park, old buildings and signage, and the magnificent neon prospector on the Hotel Nevada's Nevada Club.\nSurrounding the city are mountain ranges that offer the adventure of driving a 4-wheel drive vehicle over beautiful mountains, up canyons, and remote valleys.\nMountain climbers can find many peaks over 11,000 feet in elevation.\nGo next.\nEly is at the crossroads to several destinations throughout the western United States. Idaho is accessible via US 93 north, while US 50 and US 6 lead to points in California, Utah and Colorado. The section of US Highway 50 heading west out of Ely is known as \"The Loneliest Road in America.\" Central Nevada is filled with desert valleys between sets of mountain ridges. As Highway 50 crosses each of these gently curved valleys in a perfectly straight course, you can often see 20 or 30 miles of completely empty road ahead of you. You just must make sure that you have a car in good working condition as there are basically no services for more than 50 miles at most times and summer temperatures can easily reach 110 F, and winter temperatures getting well below 0 F.\nLas Vegas is approximately four hours away on US 93 southbound, but one can take a shortcut via Nevada State Highway 318, which begins at an intersection with US 6 about 30 miles outside Ely. Taking this route shortens the trip to Vegas by about 60 miles (100 km), but the road is extremely desolate with nearly nonexistent cell phone coverage and no services to speak of apart from the small town of Lund. Don't try it unless you trust your car and you have emergency supplies."}
{"id": "10903", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10903", "title": "Elyria", "text": "There is more than one place called Elyria:"}
{"id": "10904", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10904", "title": "Elyria (Ohio)", "text": "Elyria is a city in Northeast Ohio. It is the county seat of Lorain County. Elyria sits at the forks of the Black River 23 mi southwest of Cleveland. The city is home to Lorain County Community College.\nUnderstand.\nThe city's name is derived from the surname of its founder, Heman Ely, and Illyria, the historical name used by ancient Greeks and Romans to refer to the western Balkans.\nIn 2010, the city had a population of 55,000. \nHistory.\nThe village of Elyria was founded in 1817 by Heman Ely, who built a log house, dam, gristmill and sawmill on the village's site along the Black River. Ely began to build more houses to accommodate European-American settlers migrating there. By the time Ely died in 1852, Elyria had five churches, three grocery stores, three flour mills, a newspaper, and a population of more than 1,500. \nIn the first half of the 20th century, the town developed some manufacturing, and a range of retail businesses.\nIn 1967, at the peak of Elyria's population, Midway Mall was opened. It changed the local economy by attracting local businesses from the town center or causing so much competition they went out of business. Industrial restructuring meant that good jobs left the area, and poverty increased. Three major car plant closings in the area lead to economic stagnation and joblessness in the 1970s and 1980s that affected numerous communities. The region was nicknamed \"the Rustbelt,\" suggesting the decline of its former industries.\nIn the 1990s, Elyria experienced a minor revival with construction of some new roads and housing. It serves as a residential, suburban \"bedroom community\" for Cleveland, to the east, where new businesses and industries are developing with an increase in new jobs. Hard times and new ideas hit Elyria as the 2000s and 2010s rolled on, as industries such as Bendix, Riddell, and 3M took their business elsewhere, outside the city. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nOH-2, OH-57, OH-113, OH-301, US-20, I-80/Ohio Turnpike, and I-90."}
{"id": "10905", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10905", "title": "El Yunque National Forest", "text": "El Yunque National Forest, in the Eastern Coast region of Puerto Rico, is the only tropical rainforest in the U.S. National Forest System.\nUnderstand.\nOnce you arrive at El Yunque National Forest be sure to stop by El Portal Visitor Center before hitting the trails. Take some time to grab some water, if you don't already have some, and a map while you're there. Make sure that you bring a bathing suit, a towel, some sunblock, and insect repellent.\nHistory.\nEl Yunque National Forest, formerly known as the Caribbean National Forest, is in the rugged Sierra de Luquillo, 40 km southeast of San Juan. The forest covers lands of the municipalities of Can\u00f3vanas, Las Piedras, Luquillo, Fajardo, Ceiba, Naguabo, and R\u00edo Grande. It gets its name from an Indian spirit Yuquiye (which means \"Forest of Clouds\") that gave the mountain that dominates the 28,000 acres of tropical forest.\nThe forest was set aside in 1876 by the Spanish Crown, and represents one of the oldest reserves in the Western Hemisphere. \nThe over 240 species (26 species found nowhere else) of trees and plants cause the government of Puerto Rico to spend a great deal of money to preserve floral species and animals that are on the verge of extinction. The total area is 11,270 ha (75% of Puerto Rico's virgin forest is here).\nLandscape.\nThe landscape varies (see below) but is generally very steep and thickly forested, ranging from lowland rainforest (though not the giant trees and wild swamps found in rainforests further south) to stunted cloud forest and rocky cliffs at the top. The peak of El Yunque tops out at 1000 m (3200 ft).\nFlora and fauna.\nThe ecosystem of El Yunque is held in place by the remaining protected old growth forest. Deforested areas around the region have generally been taken over by invasive grasses, the prevent the recolonization of the area by trees. \nClimate.\nEl Yunque National Forest is a cool, mountainous, and sub-tropical rainforest. The eastern portion of the Luquillo mountains get the most rain. El Yunque is the rainiest of all the National Forests with up to 240 inches per year. More than 100 billion gallons (380 billion L) of rainwater fall on the forest per year. The climate is frost-free and ranges in moisture from semi-desert to rain forest conditions within very short distances. There are strong easterly trade-winds and cool weather is normal at the higher elevations. The mountains create their own weather, which changes rapidly. If you see clouds at the top as you arrive, it will rain. If you don't, it will probably rain anyway.\nGet in.\nConsidering arranging for a rental car. You can enjoy a day at the rainforest without a car, but you'll be severely limited in terms of going off the beaten path or spending any extra time at the park. The drive from San Juan to El Yunque will take about an hour regardless of which one of the most common routes you take. From San Juan, all of them will take you along Route 3 for the majority of the trip, so head east on it until you reach Route 191. Transitioning from Route 3 to Route 191 will take you through the small town of Palmer in R\u00edo Grande.\nFees and permits.\nThere are no entrance fees to visit the forest, you just drive up, though El Portal Visitors Center cost $4 per person. A Forest Service one-hour guided tour cost a $5 donation.\nGet around.\nGroup tours will show you the animals, caves, and water falls, but trails are open to self-guided visitors. Consider wearing boots or shoes with a bit of ankle support - while most of the trails in the forest are fairly well worn it is still uneven terrain and a sprained ankle is no fun no matter where you are. Most trails are steep and wet, but well maintained.\nDo.\nYou can hike, go kayaking and guided scuba diving.\nEat.\nYou won't be able to find restaurants in the rainforest. It's a good idea to bring food with you. There are many restaurants at the foot of the rainforest.\nSleep.\nCamping is generally prohibited in order to protect wildlife, and would be unpleasant in the rain.\nStay safe.\nEl Yunque lacks large or dangerous wildlife. The main dangers are slips and falls on wet trails and streams, but the forest does have a few poisonous plants, notably a giant stinging nettle (\"ortiga\") which will do no permanent damage but is much more painful than mainland nettles; you can identify it by the warts and hairs on its large and broad leaves. Stay alert for the different animals and insects that you come close to."}
{"id": "10906", "revid": "2350368", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10906", "title": "Emas National Park", "text": "Emas National Park is in Centre West of Brazil. It is a UNESCO World Heritage site due to its cerrado savannah, which is one of the oldest and most diverse ecosystems in the world.\nUnderstand.\nEmas National Park protects one of the largest and best-preserved expanses of Cerrado savanna in central Brazil. It covers about 132,788 hectares across the municipalities of Mineiros and Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u in Goi\u00e1s and Costa Rica in Mato Grosso do Sul. Its name refers to the greater rhea\u2014\"ema\" in Portuguese\u2014a large flightless bird frequently seen in the park\u2019s open grasslands. \nThe park forms part of the UNESCO-listed Cerrado Protected Areas, together with Chapada dos Veadeiros National Park. UNESCO recognizes these parks for preserving representative Cerrado habitats and populations of threatened animals such as giant anteaters, giant armadillos, maned wolves, jaguars and pampas deer. The region also contributes to aquifer recharge and feeds watercourses connected with both the Amazon and Paran\u00e1\u2013La Plata drainage systems. \nThe surrounding countryside is dominated by large, highly mechanized farms producing soybeans, maize and other crops. The sudden contrast between natural grassland inside the park and geometric fields outside its boundary is particularly visible from entrance roads and aerial views. Agriculture supports the regional economy but has left Emas increasingly isolated from other natural Cerrado areas. UNESCO describes it as almost completely surrounded by agricultural land and identifies agricultural encroachment, invasive grasses and fires spreading from surrounding areas as continuing management concerns. \nBiological-corridor projects seek to maintain links between Emas, the upper Taquari basin, the Pantanal and other Cerrado remnants. These connections are especially important for jaguars, pumas, maned wolves and other animals that move across territories much larger than the park itself.\nHistory.\nEmas National Park was created on 11 January 1961 by Decree 49,874. The original decree protected land around the headwaters and upper courses of the Correntes, Apor\u00e9 and Sucuri\u00fa rivers, primarily in Goi\u00e1s but also extending into what was then the state of Mato Grosso. \nIts establishment came during a period when the interior of Brazil was being opened for roads, settlement and agricultural development. At the time, extensive natural grasslands and savannas still covered much of southwestern Goi\u00e1s. The park was intended to preserve a representative area of this landscape and its wildlife before large-scale farming transformed the region.\nThe boundaries were revised by Decree 70,375 of 6 April 1972. The revision formally described an area of approximately 100,000 hectares and removed about 50,000 hectares from the original protected territory. Later surveying and boundary adjustments produced the present official area of approximately 132,788 hectares. \nDuring the late 20th century, soybean cultivation and other mechanized agriculture expanded rapidly around the park. Natural Cerrado outside the protected boundary was progressively converted into fields and pasture, increasing concerns about habitat isolation, pesticide drift, invasive plants and escaped agricultural fires.\nIn 2001, UNESCO inscribed Emas and Chapada dos Veadeiros together on the World Heritage List as the Cerrado Protected Areas. The designation recognized their importance as refuges for rare and endemic species and as examples of the ecological processes that shaped one of the world\u2019s oldest and most biologically diverse tropical ecosystems. \nFire has always been part of Cerrado ecology, but unusually extensive or frequent burns can cause severe damage. A major wildfire in 2010 affected approximately 90% of Emas National Park, emphasizing the need for firebreaks, trained brigades, controlled burning and cooperation with surrounding farms. \nLandscape.\nEmas lies on the broad, gently rolling Serra dos Caiap\u00f3s plateau. Unlike Brazil\u2019s more mountainous national parks, most of the terrain is level or only slightly undulating, producing immense horizons and long views across open savanna. Elevations are generally around 700\u20131,000 m above sea level.\nThe landscape contains a mosaic of Cerrado habitats. Vast areas of campo limpo consist mainly of grasses and low herbs, while \"campo sujo\" has scattered shrubs. Denser \"cerrado sensu stricto\" includes twisted, thick-barked trees adapted to seasonal drought, acidic soils and periodic fire. Narrow gallery forests follow rivers and streams, and palm-filled wetlands known as veredas develop around springs and waterlogged ground.\nThousands of termite mounds rise above the grasslands and are among the park\u2019s most distinctive landscape features. During a short period after the first rains, some become illuminated by the green glow of beetle larvae feeding on insects attracted to their light. The mounds also provide shelter and feeding sites for anteaters, armadillos, reptiles and many smaller animals.\nThe park occupies an important watershed. Streams in and around Emas contribute to the headwaters of the Araguaia\u2013Tocantins, Paran\u00e1 and Paraguay river systems, linking its uplands ecologically with both the Amazon basin and the Pantanal. UNESCO identifies the area as important for groundwater recharge and for sustaining watercourses beyond the park boundary. \nGallery forests and waterways such as the Formoso River break up the otherwise open terrain. Clear rivers, marshes and seasonal wetlands provide habitat for tapirs, marsh deer, capybaras, waterbirds and aquatic wildlife.\nOutside the boundary, the natural savanna gives way abruptly to soybean plantations, maize fields, pasture and farm roads. This sharp edge makes the park resemble an island of native Cerrado within an agricultural landscape and helps explain the emphasis placed on biological corridors and cooperation with neighboring landowners.\nFlora and fauna.\nEmas National Park shows a typical cerrado ecosystem: a treeless savannah with tall termite mounds and an interesting amount of wildlife: the giant anteater, the maned wolf, giant armadillo, pampas deer, and the greater rhea, among others.\nEmas National Park also holds a small jaguar population, perhaps consisting of about 10-12 animals. Only about 40% of the reserve, which covers 1,320 km\u00b2 (510 sq mi) in total, is good jaguar habitat.\nOther larger mammals include puma, ocelot, Brazilian tapir, collared peccary, white-lipped peccary, marsh deer, red brocket, gray brocket, black howler monkey and capybara.\nClimate.\nIt has a tropical, hot climate, ranging from humid to semi-arid, with up to five months of drought each year.\nGet in.\nChapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u is the closest municipality to the park, approximately 25 km away. It has services such as banking, mechanics, pharmacists, and mail. Mineiros, the main urban center in the region, 110 km away from the headquarters of the national park is a larger town with medical service. \nFees and permits.\nAdmission to Emas National Park is free for all visitors, and ordinary daytime visits do not require advance booking. The park normally opens daily from 8AM to 5PM, although the Bandeira and Jacuba gates close from noon to 1PM. During the seasonal bioluminescence period, opening may be extended until 9PM. \nSome activities require advance booking and an accredited environmental guide, including camping, overnight stays, vehicle safaris, rafting, tubing, access to restricted sectors and visits to the observation tower. Special guided activities may be authorized between 5AM and 10PM. The guide, equipment and activity charges are separate from park admission. \nFree self-guided trails may be used without a guide where signs and park rules explicitly permit this. Nevertheless, visitors planning a long drive, nighttime visit or specialist wildlife excursion should contact the park beforehand to confirm current access, fire restrictions and seasonal closures. The official contact is +55 64 99972-8176 or parquenacionaldasemas@icmbio.gov.br. \nOnline scheduling is available through Brazil\u2019s federal conservation-area service for activities that require reservations. Applicants provide their identification, visit date and intended attraction; costs vary according to the selected service. \nGet around.\nThe park is large and is mainly explored by private vehicle, guided safari vehicle, bicycle or on foot. There is no public transportation between the gates, trails, rivers and visitor areas. Visitors without their own transport should arrange a guided excursion from Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u, Mineiros or Costa Rica.\nThe generally level plateau makes driving easier than in mountainous national parks, but most internal roads are unpaved. A high-clearance vehicle is advisable, and a 4\u00d74 may be necessary after heavy rain. Carry a spare tyre, tools, drinking water and enough fuel for the complete outing. Do not rely on mobile reception or roadside services.\nSelf-guided walking is allowed only on designated routes and during permitted hours. Other areas require an accredited guide for safety and conservation reasons. Wildlife can be observed from both the road and authorized walking trails, but visitors should leave the vehicle only where park staff or signs permit it. \nCyclists may bring their own bicycle, and rental bicycles have traditionally been available around the Bandeira and Jacuba gates. The Jacubinha and Formoso\u2013Gl\u00f3ria routes provide access to streams and river scenery, although current rental availability and trail conditions should be checked before arrival. \nGuided safari vehicles can carry groups through a broader section of the park. Official visitor information describes excursions lasting as long as eight hours, accompanied by a guide. Allow a full day rather than trying to cross the park quickly. \nSee.\nThe park protects an exceptionally complete sample of the Cerrado, including open grassland, scrubby savanna, wetlands, palm-lined \"veredas\" and gallery forest. Its broad, nearly level plateaus provide expansive views that are unusual in more densely wooded Brazilian parks. \nWildlife commonly sought by visitors includes greater rheas, giant anteaters, maned wolves, pampas deer, marsh deer, tapirs and crab-eating foxes. Jaguars, pumas, giant armadillos and other threatened species are present but much more difficult to see. Early morning and late afternoon usually provide the best viewing. \nThousands of large termite mounds rise across the grasslands and are among the park\u2019s most characteristic sights. Around October and November, depending on rain and insect activity, some mounds become covered in greenish points of light produced by beetle larvae that attract flying prey. This celebrated bioluminescence is visible after nightfall and should be visited through an authorized guided excursion. \nThe Rio Formoso and other protected waterways provide a contrast to the dry grasslands, with clear water, rapids, riparian vegetation and wildlife tracks along their banks. The park also protects streams connected with the Taquari and Araguaia river basins. \nAn observation tower and selected viewpoints provide elevated views over the Cerrado. Access to the tower requires an accredited guide, and availability may depend on staffing, weather and maintenance. \nDo.\nTake a wildlife drive through the open grasslands, where large mammals and birds are generally easier to spot than in dense forest. A guided safari is particularly useful for locating maned wolves, giant anteaters and less obvious bird species.\nWalk one of the park\u2019s free self-guided trails, remaining within the marked area and permitted hours. Move quietly and examine the smaller features of the Cerrado, including wildflowers, insects, tracks and the numerous termite mounds. \nGo cycling on designated roads and trails. The predominantly level terrain is well suited to longer rides, although heat, dust, wind and the absence of shade can make cycling tiring. Bring repair equipment and more water than expected. \nJoin an authorized boat or floating excursion on the Rio Formoso. Guided rafting trips last approximately two hours and may include opportunities to enter the water at places selected by the instructor. A faster tubing or \"boia-cross\" descent uses a section with stronger rapids and lasts approximately 40 minutes. Both activities require an approved operator and appropriate safety equipment. \nArrange a nighttime visit during the bioluminescence season. The spectacle is dependent on rainfall, insect life cycles and darkness, so exact dates and intensity cannot be guaranteed. Confirm the season with the park or a local guide rather than organizing a trip solely around an old calendar. \nThe park is also excellent for birdwatching, nature photography and stargazing. Large open areas support rheas, seriemas, raptors and threatened grassland birds, while the limited artificial lighting produces dark night skies.\nBuy.\nShopping inside the park is very limited. There is no dependable general store, fuel station, pharmacy or permanent souvenir shop serving every visitor sector. Purchase food, water, medicines, sunscreen, insect repellent and other supplies in Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u, Mineiros or Costa Rica before entering.\nAuthorized vendors may sell food, drinks or limited local products at Espa\u00e7o Formoso, particularly during events and busier periods. A restaurant, camping infrastructure and additional bathrooms were developed there, but ICMBio notes that demand is seasonal and food service may not operate permanently. Do not arrive without your own provisions unless service has been confirmed. \nLocally produced Cerrado honey, preserves, handicrafts and regional foods may be available in the surrounding towns. Do not buy animals, feathers, eggs, plants, skins or anything claimed to have been collected within the national park.\nEat.\nFood service inside Emas National Park is limited and may be seasonal or event-based. Espa\u00e7o Formoso has a restaurant structure designed for authorized food vendors, but ICMBio has stated that the park does not yet receive enough visitors to guarantee permanent operation. Confirm current opening before relying on it. \nMost day visitors should bring a packed breakfast or lunch, snacks and emergency food from Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u, Mineiros or Costa Rica. Guided operators may provide meals or packed lunches as part of a safari, river trip or camping package.\nStore food inside closed containers and never feed wildlife. Rheas, foxes and other animals may become dangerous or dependent on people when deliberately fed. Remove all packaging, food scraps and other rubbish from the park. Authorized food providers are required to manage their waste and may not feed or disturb wildlife. \nDrink.\nBring enough drinking water for the entire visit, with an additional emergency supply for delays or vehicle trouble. The exposed grasslands can become extremely hot, and many routes provide little shade.\nDo not rely on purchasing bottled water inside the park. Non-alcoholic drinks may be sold by authorized vendors at Espa\u00e7o Formoso when food service is operating, but availability is not guaranteed. \nWater from the Rio Formoso, streams or park taps should not be consumed unless staff confirm that it has been treated. Natural water should be filtered and disinfected or boiled before drinking.\nAlcohol is inappropriate before driving, cycling, rafting, tubing or wildlife activities. Authorized food sales described by ICMBio are limited to food and non-alcoholic beverages. \nSleep.\nThere is no conventional hotel or lodge inside Emas National Park. Most visitors stay in Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u, Mineiros or Costa Rica and enter the park on a day trip or as part of a prearranged guided excursion.\nChapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u is the closest practical base for the Bandeira entrance and usually offers the shortest morning transfer. Mineiros has a larger range of urban services and is convenient for the Jacuba entrance, while Costa Rica is useful for approaching the park from Mato Grosso do Sul.\nVisitors planning an overnight stay within the protected area must arrange it beforehand with an ICMBio-authorized guide. The park specifically lists overnight stays and camping among the activities requiring an accredited guide for safety and conservation reasons. \nLodging.\nHotels, small guesthouses and rental accommodation are available in the surrounding towns. Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u is generally the most convenient base for wildlife drives, bioluminescence excursions and activities around the Rio Formoso. Mineiros is farther away but has a broader selection of shops, restaurants, vehicle services and accommodation.\nICMBio\u2019s visitor page lists Pousada do Gl\u00f3ria, beside the road connecting Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u and Costa Rica, as an accommodation option near the park. It also lists several hotels in Mineiros and Chapad\u00e3o do C\u00e9u, but travellers should confirm that individual properties are still operating and verify current rates and road access before travelling. \nAccommodation near the park may be simple and have limited mobile reception. Confirm whether meals, drinking water, air conditioning, mosquito screens and guided transfers are included.\nCamping.\nA designated camping area operates within the park\u2019s visitor structure, with toilets, changing facilities, environmental-education space and picnic shelters reported around the Costa Rica and Rio Formoso sector. Camping is not an informal drop-in activity: visitors must book beforehand and be accompanied by an authorized environmental guide. \nBring a suitable tent, sleeping mat, warm layer, rain protection, insect repellent, headlamp, drinking water and all necessary food. Confirm whether cooking areas, showers, electricity, firewood or refrigeration are available rather than assuming that they will be provided.\nThe open Cerrado offers little protection from wind, intense sun or thunderstorms. Do not pitch a tent beneath isolated trees during lightning, beside a watercourse that may rise after rain, or outside the area identified by the guide.\nCampfires may be prohibited because of wildfire risk. Use only authorized cooking equipment and follow all instructions from ICMBio and the guide. Carry every piece of rubbish out of the park.\nBackcountry.\nThere is no unrestricted backcountry-camping system. Overnight stays away from the established visitor area require explicit authorization, an accredited guide and a route approved by park management. Some sectors are restricted for wildlife protection, scientific research, fire management or visitor safety. \nDo not leave the road or establish a wilderness camp independently. The landscape may appear open, but tall grass, wetlands and gallery forest can conceal wildlife, holes, streams and changing terrain. Mobile reception is unreliable, and rescue assistance may be far away.\nAn authorized expedition should be self-sufficient in food, drinking water, shelter, navigation, first aid and emergency communications. Avoid contaminating natural water sources, disturbing termite mounds or approaching wildlife resting near a proposed campsite.\nStay safe.\nThe park is remote and has long stretches without fuel, shops, medical care or dependable mobile reception. Begin with a full tank, carry a spare tyre, tools, food and substantially more drinking water than expected. Tell the guide or accommodation which route is planned and when the group expects to return.\nActivities such as overnight stays, camping, safaris, boating, tubing and access to particular areas require an authorized guide. Follow the guide\u2019s instructions and remain within the permitted area and operating hours. \nWild animals include jaguars, pumas, maned wolves, tapirs, giant anteaters, wild pigs and several species of deer. Most avoid people, but they should never be followed, surrounded or approached for photographs. Remain inside the vehicle unless the guide identifies a safe place to leave it, and never feed wildlife.\nGiant anteaters have poor eyesight but powerful claws and may defend themselves when surprised or cornered. Give them ample room, particularly females carrying young. Avoid walking through tall grass without a guide, where animals and venomous snakes may be difficult to see.\nThe exposed Cerrado becomes hot, dry and intensely sunny. Carry a hat, sunscreen and sufficient water, and schedule strenuous activities for the cooler morning or late afternoon. Heat exhaustion and dehydration can develop even when travelling mainly by vehicle.\nThe dry season generally extends from June through September, when vegetation can burn rapidly. Fires may be caused by human activity or lightning, particularly around the transition into the rainy season. Obey closures, never discard cigarettes and leave immediately when instructed by park staff or firefighters. \nDuring the rainy season, sudden thunderstorms can bring lightning, strong wind, mud and rapidly rising streams. Avoid isolated trees and observation towers during electrical storms. Unpaved roads can become slippery or temporarily impassable, so check conditions before entering.\nUse insect repellent and covering clothing against mosquitoes, ticks and other biting insects. Brazil\u2019s Ministry of Health recommends yellow-fever vaccination for travellers visiting areas where vaccination is advised, normally at least ten days before travel. \nDo not drink untreated water from rivers, streams or park taps. Wear a life jacket during boating, floating or tubing activities and enter the Rio Formoso only where the authorized instructor permits it."}
